1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,480
The last section of this chapter deals with embolism,

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,940
and embolism is technically defined as an intravascular mass

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,020
that travels through the bloodstream

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,900
and then occludes a blood vessel.

5
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,900
And so this is the technical definition of embolism.

6
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:16,920
And, of course, the symptoms are going to depend

7
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:20,000
on the blood vessel that is involved.

8
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,460
Now, the reason why I have this in this chapter

9
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:24,860
is because the most common source of embolism

10
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:28,320
is actually a thrombus, and that's called a thromboembolist.

11
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:29,720
So the patient gets a thrombus.

12
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:33,060
The thrombus breaks away and then floats down the bloodstream

13
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,820
and then lodges someplace else.

14
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,680
That's called a thromboembolist.

15
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:38,920
And, again, this is the most common type of embolist,

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,940
and so, therefore, I place this discussion

17
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:42,100
at the end of this chapter.

18
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,200
Another type of embolist that can occur

19
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,160
is called an atherosclerotic embolist.

20
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:49,440
And the idea here is that the patient has atherosclerosis.

21
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,940
So imagine that there's an atherosclerotic plaque

22
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:53,100
here in the blood vessel,

23
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,200
and a piece of this atherosclerotic plaque breaks off

24
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:58,380
and then travels down the blood vessel

25
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:00,180
and then lodges someplace else.

26
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:02,780
So that's called an atherosclerotic embolist.

27
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,680
And, again, it's due to dislodging of a plaque.

28
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,700
And when you look at the embolist,

29
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:11,880
the way by which you know that it arose from atherosclerosis

30
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,200
or that it's a piece of atherosclerosis, essentially,

31
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:18,080
is that you'll see cholesterol clefts in the embolist.

32
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,660
And that's key, cholesterol clefts in the embolist.

33
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:21,680
So here's a picture.

34
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:22,780
This is a blood vessel.

35
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:26,480
And you can see that this blood vessel is occluded here

36
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,320
by this embolist.

37
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:30,240
And when we look at this embolist,

38
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,600
one of the things that we recognize

39
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,260
is that there are these cholesterol crystals

40
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:35,380
here in the middle of it,

41
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,000
indicating that this must have arisen from atherosclerosis

42
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,700
that dislodged and then traveled down the bloodstream

43
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:43,120
to occlude the blood supply.

44
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:46,080
Another type of embolist that we can have is a fat embolist.

45
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:48,600
And this is where you would crack open a bone, for example.

46
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,680
So it's classically associated with trauma and bone fracture

47
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:53,540
or soft tissue could get damaged.

48
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:56,340
This would release fat into the blood supply,

49
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:58,560
which could then lodge someplace else.

50
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,500
Now, this classically develops while there is a fracture.

51
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:02,680
So the patient gets a fracture

52
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,620
and then shortly thereafter gets a fat embolist

53
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:07,060
or shortly after repair of a fracture.

54
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,500
So, you know, within a short period of time after repair.

55
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,380
The classic findings are that the patient has dyspnea

56
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:16,740
along with petechia on the skin overlying the chest.

57
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:19,580
And the reason they get dyspnea is because the classic location

58
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:22,340
for a fat embolist is the blood vessels of the lung.

59
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,980
So the fat basically goes to the blood vessels of the lung

60
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,040
and then gets lodged in those blood vessels.

61
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:29,260
Here's a classic picture of a fat embolist.

62
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,260
Clearly, this is a blood vessel here.

63
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:34,540
This center area represents the lumen of the blood vessel.

64
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:36,860
And we can see that this lumen is occluded

65
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:41,080
by all these circular spaces, which basically represent fat.

66
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,500
So this would be an example of a fat embolist.

67
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:46,560
Another type of embolist that can arise within the bloodstream

68
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,000
is called a gas embolist.

69
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:51,360
This is classically seen in something called decompression sickness.

70
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,000
Now, the idea here is that a person goes diving.

71
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:58,420
And when they go diving, you know that the pressure as they dive,

72
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:00,260
it increases as the lower they go.

73
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,360
So as they slowly descend, there's increased pressure

74
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,720
and that forces nitrogen to dissolve in the blood.

75
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:09,900
Now, when the diver comes up, they rapidly ascend.

76
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:13,920
And when they rapidly ascend, the nitrogen can actually precipitate

77
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:16,280
out of the blood as small little gas bubbles,

78
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:18,540
which can then lodge in various tissues,

79
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,580
creating problems of the gas embolist.

80
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:25,120
So, for example, patients can get joint and muscle pain

81
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:28,780
because the nitrogen bubbles would lodge within the joints and the muscles.

82
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:33,380
And also, the nitrogen gas can embolize to the lung,

83
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,220
giving the patient respiratory symptoms.

84
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:38,120
And when the patient has joint and muscle pain,

85
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,240
that's classically called the BENS, B-E-N-D-S.

86
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:42,860
And when they have respiratory symptoms,

87
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:46,700
that's classically called the CHOKES, C-H-O-K-E-S.

88
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,780
Now, there's a chronic form of this disease called Kaysan's disease

89
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:54,400
in which patients have chronic gas emboli due to nitrogen.

90
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:56,020
And in this particular case,

91
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:58,460
those gas emboli would cause infarction of the bone.

92
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:02,160
And so they get multifocal ischemic necrosis of the bone.

93
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:03,980
And that's called Kaysan's disease.

94
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:08,220
Gas emboli can also arise when a patient undergoes laparoscopic surgery.

95
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,000
Remember that during laparoscopic surgery,

96
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:11,500
we pump air into the abdomen,

97
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:14,400
and it's possible that some of that air could get into the bloodstream

98
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:16,660
and then subsequently result in an embolus.

99
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:20,020
The next type of embolus is called an amniotic fluid embolus.

100
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:22,320
Obviously, this would be a woman who's pregnant.

101
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,420
The amniotic fluid enters a maternal circulation

102
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:29,700
and then lodges within the mom's lungs most often.

103
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:33,200
Usually, this occurs during delivery or during labor.

104
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:35,140
As I've stated here in your text,

105
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,740
the classic presentation is that of shortness of breath.

106
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:41,280
That's because you get the emboli within the lung.

107
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:44,980
You get neurologic symptoms because of the emboli going to the brain.

108
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:46,620
And the patients also get DIC.

109
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:50,360
And remember that the amniotic fluid is loaded with tissue thromboplastin.

110
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:51,180
That's high yield.

111
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:54,280
The amniotic fluid is loaded with tissue thromboplastin.

112
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:57,740
And tissue thromboplastin has the ability to activate the coag cascade,

113
00:04:58,140 --> 00:04:59,520
which can then result in DIC.

114
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:02,700
When you look at the emboli in the patient's lungs,

115
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,620
so you're looking at mom's lung and you notice that there are emboli there,

116
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:10,560
the characteristic finding in those emboli will be squamous cells and keratin debris.

117
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:14,900
And the idea here is that remember that the baby was floating around in the amniotic fluid,

118
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:19,220
and so the baby's skin was sloughing off over time in that amniotic fluid,

119
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:23,500
and the amniotic fluid will therefore be loaded with squamous cells and keratin debris.

120
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:25,580
And this is what it looks like.

121
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:30,940
Here is an alveolar air sac, and this is the interstitium with a blood vessel.

122
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:34,780
And we can clearly see that within this circular space of the blood vessel,

123
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:36,960
there is all this keratin debris.

124
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:39,980
And so when a patient has this keratin debris within the blood vessels,

125
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:42,820
we think about an amniotic fluid embolus.

126
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:47,960
One disorder that can arise with embolus is called pulmonary embolus,

127
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:52,000
and this is usually due to a thromboembolus that arises from a DVT.

128
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:54,820
Now the idea here is that a patient gets a deep vein thrombosis,

129
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,820
for example, usually of the lower extremity,

130
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,960
usually involving the femoral, iliac, or popliteal veins,

131
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:05,600
and that deep vein thrombosis can actually dislodge,

132
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:07,820
and when it dislodges, you'd get a thromboembolus,

133
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:11,680
and that thromboembolus would eventually enter the pulmonary circulation,

134
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:13,780
and when it enters the pulmonary circulation,

135
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:17,460
it can cause something called a pulmonary embolus.

136
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:20,680
Now most often, when patients get pulmonary embolus, or PE,

137
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:23,440
it's clinically silent, and that's for two reasons.

138
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,380
Number one, the lung has a dual blood supply.

139
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:29,200
Remember that the pulmonary artery is going into the lung,

140
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:30,760
so that's one blood supply,

141
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:33,620
and then you've also got the bronchial artery coming off the aorta

142
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,120
that also goes to the lung,

143
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:37,820
which essentially keeps the lung tissue alive and healthy,

144
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:39,700
and so that's a second blood supply.

145
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:43,500
So even if you were to occlude a small area of the lung,

146
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:46,140
the second blood supply would be able to sustain the lung.

147
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:49,400
So that's one reason why PE is usually clinically silent.

148
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:51,600
Another reason why PE is clinically silent

149
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:53,400
is because most emboli are usually small,

150
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:56,100
and as soon as the embolus lodges within the lung,

151
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:58,080
it self-resolves and gets lysed.

152
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:02,520
So these are the two reasons why PE is most often clinically silent.

153
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:05,720
It is possible, however, as a complication of PE,

154
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:07,780
to get an area of infarction within the lung.

155
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:10,240
That's usually going to occur when you have a couple things.

156
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:13,500
Number one, you need obstruction of a large or medium-sized artery.

157
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,440
So that's really the first thing we need.

158
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:18,180
You've got to have a big embolus that's going to obstruct

159
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:20,140
either a large or medium-sized artery.

160
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:22,440
And number two, the patient needs to have

161
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,300
some sort of pre-existing cardiopulmonary compromise.

162
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:28,500
Now by having pre-existing cardiopulmonary compromise,

163
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:31,600
the lung is susceptible to this type of damage,

164
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:34,060
and therefore having the dual blood supply

165
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:37,840
would not be as protective as it would be in a normal individual.

166
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,820
So again, two things before you're going to see infarction.

167
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,780
You're going to need a large embolus,

168
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:44,440
and it's usually going to be a patient

169
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:46,780
with pre-existing cardiopulmonary compromise.

170
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,640
Only about 10% of PEs actually cause pulmonary infarction.

171
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:53,900
Clinically, when patients get pulmonary infarction,

172
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:56,340
then they're going to present with classic signs

173
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:59,520
of shortness of breath, hemoptysis, pleuritic chest pain,

174
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:00,420
pleural effusion.

175
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:02,300
So these are some of the signs and symptoms

176
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:03,960
that alert us that we might be dealing

177
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,520
with a pulmonary infarction due to a PE.

178
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,920
A VQ scan would show mismatch.

179
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:10,780
Remember that the V represents ventilation

180
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:12,320
and the Q represents perfusion.

181
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:15,160
And of course, if we block the blood supply with a PE,

182
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:17,620
then the perfusion is going to be abnormal.

183
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,300
At the same time, one of the tests that's useful

184
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:21,680
is something called a spiral CT,

185
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:23,760
and the spiral CT would show

186
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,240
that there was a vascular filling defect in the lung,

187
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,600
indicating that we've got some sort of embolus

188
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,840
that's blocking the blood supply within the lung.

189
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:33,660
Additionally, you can do a Doppler ultrasound

190
00:08:33,660 --> 00:08:34,800
to the lower extremity,

191
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:36,780
and that would help to identify the source.

192
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:39,000
It would help you to identify that a DVT is present.

193
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:41,600
And finally, the D-dimer is going to be elevated.

194
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:44,120
And the reason why the D-dimer is elevated

195
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:46,400
is because the patient's lysing both the PE

196
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:48,540
as well as the DVT,

197
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:51,140
and so that usually is going to elevate the D-dimer.

198
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:53,000
Because remember, any time you cut up

199
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,920
a platelet fibrin thrombus,

200
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:56,940
that's going to result in D-dimer

201
00:08:56,940 --> 00:08:58,280
being pushed out into the blood.

202
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:00,400
So the D-dimer is often elevated.

203
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:03,400
On gross exam, when you have a pulmonary infarction,

204
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:07,220
you classically see a hemorrhagic wedge-shaped infarct.

205
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:09,580
Now, the reason why you see a wedge-shaped infarct

206
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:11,260
is the following.

207
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:12,980
Remember that the blood supply in the lung

208
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:14,500
is dichotomously branching,

209
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:17,540
so that if this is the blood supply going this way,

210
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:19,320
it branches, and then it branches again,

211
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:20,380
and then it branches again.

212
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:22,100
And so if you get a PE here,

213
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:24,420
you're going to get infarction of everything

214
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:27,020
that would go through this branch and this branch,

215
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,740
which would then knock out this entire area.

216
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:32,420
And so you can see that this kind of looks like a wedge.

217
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:36,240
And we say that the wedge points to the area of occlusion.

218
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:38,580
So that's the reason why the infarction

219
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:39,520
would be wedge-shaped.

220
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:42,480
At the same time, the reason why it's hemorrhagic

221
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,300
is because you have a second blood supply

222
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:47,640
bringing blood into the dead tissue,

223
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,660
and the dead tissue is loose.

224
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:52,060
And remember that if you go back to Chapter 1

225
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:53,520
in our discussion of infarction,

226
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:55,920
we said that in order to get a hemorrhagic infarction,

227
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:56,780
you need two things.

228
00:09:56,820 --> 00:09:59,220
You need blood to re-enter the dead tissue,

229
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:02,100
and you need the dead tissue to have a loose consistency

230
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:03,520
so that it can hold the blood

231
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,540
to reveal a hemorrhagic infarction.

232
00:10:06,220 --> 00:10:07,980
And that's exactly what occurs in the lung.

233
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:10,080
You've got a second blood supply,

234
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:12,420
and you've got a loosely organized tissue.

235
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,380
Another possibility of a PE

236
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:16,080
is that the patient develops sudden death.

237
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:18,660
This is usually not seen unless you have a massive embolus.

238
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:21,360
And the classic is a large saddle embolus.

239
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:22,960
Now the reason why death occurs

240
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:25,520
is because there is electromechanical dissociation.

241
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:26,680
So the heart's pumping,

242
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:28,860
but the blood supply is totally blocked.

243
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:31,320
And so the patient undergoes sudden death.

244
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,960
And so this is what it classically looks like.

245
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:35,720
This is a saddle embolus,

246
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:36,760
and we can see here that

247
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:39,360
this is the branching of the pulmonary artery,

248
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:40,480
here and here.

249
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:42,280
And then we can see that this embolus,

250
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:43,180
as I'm drawing here,

251
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,440
has saddled and knocked out

252
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:47,040
both of the pulmonary arteries.

253
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:48,040
And so there's basically,

254
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:51,140
and the entire vascular supply is occluded,

255
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:52,880
and so the heart's going to pump against that,

256
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:55,280
but blood's not going to be able to go any further.

257
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,000
And so this is an example of a saddle embolus,

258
00:10:58,100 --> 00:10:59,720
and would classically be seen

259
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,900
in a patient who undergoes sudden death.

260
00:11:03,020 --> 00:11:04,260
One final complication,

261
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:06,220
or final consequence, I should say,

262
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:07,220
of pulmonary emboli

263
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:08,760
is that if you have chronic emboli

264
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:10,040
over a long period of time,

265
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:12,200
those emboli can be reorganized,

266
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:13,360
and when they're reorganized,

267
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:15,460
the patients develop pulmonary hypertension.

268
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:17,000
So pulmonary hypertension,

269
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:18,500
which would be high blood pressure

270
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:19,500
in the pulmonary circuit,

271
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:22,800
is a complication of chronic emboli

272
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:24,720
that get reorganized over time.

273
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:26,600
Now it is also possible

274
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:29,580
for emboli to lodge in the systemic circuit,

275
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:31,840
and when they lodge in the systemic circuit,

276
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:32,980
the most common source

277
00:11:32,980 --> 00:11:34,300
is usually a thromboembolus,

278
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:36,080
and that usually comes from the left heart.

279
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:38,380
So when you get systemic emboli,

280
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:39,980
that is, emboli that shoot off

281
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:42,020
and lodge someplace in the systemic circuit,

282
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:43,720
again, the most common source

283
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:45,460
is a thromboembolus from the left heart.

284
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:47,560
These will travel down the systemic circuit

285
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:49,040
to occlude flow to organs.

286
00:11:49,340 --> 00:11:51,440
The most common site is the lower extremities.

287
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:52,220
And with that,

288
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:53,820
we conclude our section on embolism,

289
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:55,780
and that also concludes this chapter.

